00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:39.510
human agenda, which is interesting sort of
language to use.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.350
What is that agenda and how does it develop?

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:48.820
Well, I mean really that agenda developed
as, as far as I can tell, really in the late

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:55.380
Middle Ages with the invention of the chartered
monopoly, which is kind of the protocol operation

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:02.580
and central currency that both of those were,
were invented really to prevent the rise of

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:08.400
the middle class and to observe the Lee's
feet.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.450
You know, we had the beginnings of what looked
very much like internet culture in the late

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:17.100
Middle Ages when people came back from the
Crusades and they opened up these trade routes.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.450
They had little local markets with local currencies
that were optimized for transaction.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:28.050
They were getting wealthy and that was a problem
for the aristocracy that hadn't worked in,

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.530
you know, 10 centuries.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:34.170
So they came up with a monopoly currency,
which was basically like almost like some

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:39.159
kind of a windows operating system that you
use it or you can't transact at all.

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:44.280
And it was this growth based interest based
currency that if you borrowed some, in order

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.910
to transact you had to pay back more.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.470
You know, in order, in order for the economy
to keep going.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:54.729
And instead of being able to have your own
business, you had to work for one of the king's

00:01:54.729 --> 00:02:01.420
chartered monopolies as an employee selling
your time rather than the value you created.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:09.590
So boom, the innovations, the as I really
anti human and that they disconnected workers

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.170
from the value they were creating.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:16.510
We've got brands to disconnect consumers from
the producers from whom they were buying stuff.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.620
And we got mass marketing and mass media to
really disconnect consumers from one another

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:25.590
so that we bought things instead of relating
to other people.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:31.400
Now you come to the 21st century in the digital
age, we had the possibility and this is what

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:37.840
got so many of us excited in those early Internet
days, the possibility to really retrieve all

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:44.700
of those cool peer to peer bottom up values
of medieval Islam and the Commons and the

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:45.700
co op.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.490
All of these things were starting to emerge.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:52.380
But then you know, along comes wired magazine
and the these ideas about how the Internet

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.600
is going to save the Nasdaq Stock Exchange
and and allow us to have this exponential

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.959
growth like we've never had before.

00:02:59.959 --> 00:03:06.010
And now we have a whole lot of digital companies
that are not creating tools or platforms to

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.040
help people live their lives.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:13.410
They're creating tools and platforms that
really just help corporations extract value

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:19.150
from people either in terms of their time
or their data or even their consciousness,

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.800
their choice making.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:27.200
So when I say that the digital landscape has
become anti human, really what I mean is that

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:35.430
it's extending and amplifying kind of the
most anti human are biases of corporate growth

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:41.010
based capitalism and putting them on digital
steroids and it's really hard for people to

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:46.260
really maintain any sense of coherence or
values in such a space.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:47.260
Yeah.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:50.090
So I mean there's actual technological utopianism,
right?

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:56.099
Like really truly the, the, we will be happier
and more free and self actualized.

00:03:56.099 --> 00:04:00.830
The more science and technology develop, but
then there's more of what I would call like

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:07.310
kind of a passive admiration or maybe an unquestioning
adoption of some of these technologies that's

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:14.720
not as like hard and fast about we need to
push technology and science as much as possible

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.420
because it's inherently good.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.699
But just sort of like, I'm sure that has all
of this stuff develops.

00:04:20.699 --> 00:04:26.409
It's good to use in a sort of uncritical way,
and that's where I see a lot of the less healthy

00:04:26.409 --> 00:04:30.830
sort of like adoption of technology and social
media use coming from this kind of, the default

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:35.990
position is, I'll use it unless I can very
clearly think of why not to rather than what

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:40.789
cal Newport for example, advocates, which
is let's evaluate each of these tools and

00:04:40.789 --> 00:04:44.059
technologies one by one to see what it actually
does for us.

00:04:44.059 --> 00:04:45.059
Right.

00:04:45.059 --> 00:04:50.979
I mean, I come from the early cyber culture,
which was informed by really kind of the Psychedelic,

00:04:50.979 --> 00:04:53.889
a west coast counterculture.

00:04:53.889 --> 00:04:58.870
And I remember the first time Timothy Leary,
that big LSD Guru, the first time he used

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:02.460
the Internet, he said, oh my gosh, this is
acid.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.719
You know that it's as powerful as an LSD trip
and the way it changes the way someone thinks

00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:11.460
about the world and the way everything's connected
and the access to information and people that

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.530
it gives you.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:18.849
But if the Internet is as powerful as a psychedelic,
then it's really important.

00:05:18.849 --> 00:05:24.699
What mindset and what's setting you do that
drug in, you know, if, if you have the set

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:30.180
and setting goal of I want to do something
that's going to help people connect in meaningful

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:35.740
ways and help, uh, you know, the, the planet
and other species and everybody live more

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:42.370
healthily and, uh, uh, make a more egalitarian
economy and, and increased social justice,

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:46.509
you're going to get very different effects
then if you're really just a 19 year old kid

00:05:46.509 --> 00:05:51.440
who invents something on the net and then
listens to whatever the venture capitalist

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:58.979
tells him to do, you know, so we end up with
technologies that are as powerful as drugs,

00:05:58.979 --> 00:06:48.879
but we don't recognize that they're

00:06:48.879 --> 00:06:53.770
that powerful.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:58.629
What sort of, um, advice or guidelines or
infrastructure do you suggest people at the

00:06:58.629 --> 00:07:06.839
individual level sort of employ for having
a sort of healthy diet of technology in their

00:07:06.839 --> 00:07:07.999
lives?

00:07:07.999 --> 00:07:12.159
Well, I don't know that they need

00:07:12.159 --> 00:07:13.800
any technology in their diet.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:14.800
You know what I mean?

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.649
On a certain level, it's what's a healthy
diet of work?

00:07:18.649 --> 00:07:20.770
What's a healthy diet of media?

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:30.599
I went a healthy diet of, uh, online interaction,
but, uh, almost anything, almost anything

00:07:30.599 --> 00:07:35.439
that you do through digital technology is
a substitute for something that you could

00:07:35.439 --> 00:07:37.960
be doing in real life.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:44.749
And I think that what many of us are learning
is that we're trying to live our lives at

00:07:44.749 --> 00:07:45.770
scale.

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:53.229
You know, at the same scale as a corporation
or a or a, uh, uh, you know, uh, Kardashians

00:07:53.229 --> 00:07:55.169
or something who's not a person, a cardiac
shit.

00:07:55.169 --> 00:08:02.990
Maybe there's a person in there somewhere,
but it's a lifestyle that's a mediated existence.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:07.819
And we getting engaged with people in real
life.

00:08:07.819 --> 00:08:12.259
We ended up losing the ability to make eye
contact and establish rapport and breathed

00:08:12.259 --> 00:08:13.389
with other people.

00:08:13.389 --> 00:08:17.849
So I don't, I don't really think that there's
any, you know, particularly healthy or unhealthy

00:08:17.849 --> 00:08:24.759
amount or quota or quotient of technology
as long as you have the sort of the real life

00:08:24.759 --> 00:08:31.629
experience and, and sustenance, um, for you
to be able to engage in those online experiences

00:08:31.629 --> 00:08:33.250
in a healthy way.

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:37.849
You know, for the most part, the people who
are making the platforms that we engage with

00:08:37.849 --> 00:08:41.900
each other online, they don't have our best
interests at heart.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:46.600
We all know now they go to classes at Stanford
and captology, which is, you know, how to

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.350
hypnotize people using digital technology.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:54.580
You know, that we take the, the addiction
algorithms from Las Vegas slot machines and

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.490
they're in our social media feeds to get us
to keep checking them.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:05.530
So, you know, in some sense, I feel like almost
any use of digital technology is a compromise

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:09.890
and you have to be aware that, okay, I'm now
going to compromise the integrity of this

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:10.960
interaction.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:12.340
Like the one I'm having with you.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:16.600
We're going to compromise that interaction
in order to reach more people.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:21.690
But if we're very aware of the ways in which
it's compromised that I'm not making real

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:27.240
eye contact with you, that we can't establish
rapport with one another, then we're going

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:34.010
to have to be extra compassionate and extra
conscious that this, that none of the painstakingly

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:39.220
evolve social mechanisms that we have for
establishing rapport with one another, are

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:44.520
able to, uh, to, uh, to activate when we're
using this medium.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:45.520
Yeah.

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:50.800
In other words, you and I would arguably have
a better conversation in person, but for the

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:55.880
purposes of filming this segment for my program
and we're in different locations, we've sort

00:09:55.880 --> 00:10:00.240
of made the compromise that we're going to
do it over Skype, over this video conferencing

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:05.660
technology that has, there are, that's a compromise
that we're making for what I guess we've both

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:09.570
calculated if we thought about it this deeply
is, is a sort of greater good, which is we're

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:11.140
going to get this interview out to people.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:12.140
Right.

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:17.150
And as power users, you know, I realized what
a power user is, is not someone who understands

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:22.570
necessarily how to reprogram Skype, you know,
or how to know how is our image happening.

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:28.640
A power user is someone who understands those
kind of dehumanizing compromises and knows

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:31.220
how to compensate for it, can accept it.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:32.220
You know?

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:35.490
And that's, you know, and I'm sure for you
when you pick guests or when I picked podcasts

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:41.200
to be on, I tried to get a sense of is this
someone who understands that or is this someone

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:46.650
who's a, uh, uh, I'm going to go down some
pretty dark places with because they don't,

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:52.050
uh, they're not going to be forgiving enough
of, of this medium and how it works.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:57.880
So along those lines, I mean, not to be overly
Dystopian, but also not, not to sugarcoat

00:10:57.880 --> 00:10:58.880
it.

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:03.730
What do you see as the extrapolated out five
years, 10 years, whatever.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:08.790
What do you see as the, the real practical
dangers to our world becoming more technology

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:10.690
centric trick?

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:19.070
Um, I mean the main problem with any of these
technologies is that, you know, the kid who

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:22.360
invents the tech invents it for one reason.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:27.060
But then once they take the money from the
venture capitalists, I mean, and these are

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:31.380
kids, these are college freshmen who drop
out of school, you know, with their new APP

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:32.500
or platform.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:35.130
And then they listen to these venture capitalists.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.240
Then they have two objectives.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:40.730
One is to do the APP or whatever it was that
they, they thought they were going to make.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:46.530
And now the second one is to figure out a
way for that APP to also make many billions

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.790
of dollars.

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:49.790
That's really hard.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:54.550
You know, Twitter was considered a failure
when it was making $2 billion a year, which

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:58.560
I think that's about as much money as you
could expect from 140 character messaging

00:11:58.560 --> 00:11:59.560
app.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:01.730
But you're not allowed to stop at 2 billion.

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:03.990
You have to keep growing your company.

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:08.861
So now Twitter has to pivot from a messaging
company into an advertising company, to a

00:12:08.861 --> 00:12:11.310
streaming media company, into something else.

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:16.920
You know, getting more and more, becoming
more and more extractive all along the way.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:25.920
So when I look at that trajectory, what I
see are a whole lot of CEOs and, and billionaires

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:32.010
looking to figure out how can I earn enough
money to insulate myself from the world that

00:12:32.010 --> 00:13:07.200
I'm creating by building these kinds

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:17.520
of platforms.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:21.740
You know, even Adam Smith talked about there
being three main factors of production, land,

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:23.330
labor and capital, right?

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:28.110
The land that we use, the place where we were
running our company, the Labor, the people

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:32.300
that are working to make this happen, like
all those little Uber drivers and then the

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:36.910
capital and all three have sort of an equal
place in production.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:41.940
But right now in the digital realm, capital's
the only one that has a voice.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:43.790
Capital's the only one that scales infinitely.

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:47.010
You and me, people in land don't scale infinitely.

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:51.970
And when capital's the only one that has a
voice, well your labor gets disenfranchised

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:53.930
and your land gets destroyed.

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:58.070
So we're dealing with climate change and we're
dealing with economic inequality.

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:06.990
So, unless we were really to shift, I see
those things, gifts, you know, not necessarily

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:11.120
permanently worse, but worse to a point that
people say, oh, wait a minute.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.850
This is not a problem of digital technology.

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:15.410
This is a problem of economics.

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:20.370
This is a problem of growth based corporate
capitalism that just happens to be amplified

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:22.960
and accelerated by digital technology.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:28.941
So, you know, the beauty of it, you know,
my optimistic take is that digital tech, by

00:14:28.941 --> 00:14:33.779
kind of putting capitalism on steroids is
it created something that's almost like the

00:14:33.779 --> 00:14:35.570
movie Koyaanisqatsi.

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:39.410
You know, where everything happens in fast
time and it, because it's happening so quickly

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:41.730
this time, we can sort of see it.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:47.360
I feel like most people are more aware of
the extractive nature of, of the operating

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:51.400
system, of this economic operating system
and looking for alternatives.

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:54.980
So I think we're going to start to see the
emergence of cooperatives.

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:58.820
Uh, you know, a worker owned businesses.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:03.850
We're going to see people treating resources
like Commons the same way we did, you know,

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:07.730
back in the Middle Ages when we understood
this is a precious resource.

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:12.180
And rather than creating a market for it,
we're going to guard it as a Commons and,

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:15.290
and, and enforce its use and share it.

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:22.150
So I'm actually optimistic that things are
happening clearly enough and quickly enough

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:29.720
that people are going to react, um, from a
much more human centered, uh, sustainable,

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:31.459
uh, outlook.

00:15:31.459 --> 00:15:35.250
I hope you're right and I do agree that the
speed with which it's happening does seem

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:39.279
to paint a clearer picture of the fact that,
uh, that, that it is indeed happening.

